@startuml
title S3 object access using SSE-KMS customer managed key (SINGLE ACCOUNT)

autonumber

note across
SCENARIO: single account. Here Account A OWNS both the S3 bucket
and the customer managed KMS key, and the principal also lives in
Account A. (This differs from the cross-account file, where Account A
is the consumer and Account B owns the resources.)

For cross-account: Account B would own the bucket + CMK, the CMK key
policy would name Account A's role explicitly, and Account A's IAM
policy would allow kms:Decrypt / kms:GenerateDataKey on B's key ARN.
end note

actor "User / Application" as User
participant "IAM Principal\nUser or Role\nAccount A" as Principal
participant "IAM Policy\nEvaluation Engine" as IAM
participant "Amazon S3 Bucket\nsecure-bucket\nAccount A" as S3
participant "AWS KMS" as KMS
participant "CloudTrail" as CT

== One-time setup ==

User -> Principal: Use AWS credentials

Principal -> KMS: CreateKey\nKey type = symmetric encryption key

note right of KMS
Important exam point:

For S3 SSE-KMS, use a SYMMETRIC
encryption KMS key.

S3 cannot use an asymmetric KMS key
for SSE-KMS object encryption.
end note

KMS -> KMS: Create customer managed key

Principal -> KMS: CreateAlias\nalias/s3-prod-key

Principal -> KMS: Configure key policy

note right of KMS
KMS key policy

The key policy is the primary
authorization control for a KMS key.
For a CMK you OWN this policy, so you
must grant access here explicitly.

"Resource": "*" inside a key policy means
"this key" — a key policy already scopes
to the key it is attached to.

Example key policy allowing an
application role to use the key:

{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "AllowAppRoleToUseKeyForS3",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {
        "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_A_ID:role/AppRole"
      },
      "Action": [
        "kms:Decrypt",
        "kms:GenerateDataKey"
      ],
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "kms:ViaService": "s3.REGION.amazonaws.com"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}

Note: kms:GenerateDataKey is required to
UPLOAD SSE-KMS objects; kms:Decrypt is
required to DOWNLOAD them.
end note

Principal -> Principal: Attach IAM policy

note right of Principal
IAM policy attached to user or role

This allows the application principal
to read objects from S3 and use the
KMS key for cryptographic operations.

{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "AllowReadFromS3Bucket",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": [
        "s3:GetObject"
      ],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::secure-bucket/*"
    },
    {
      "Sid": "AllowKMSDecryptForS3",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": [
        "kms:Decrypt"
      ],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:kms:REGION:ACCOUNT_A_ID:key/KEY_ID"
    }
  ]
}
end note

Principal -> S3: Configure default bucket encryption\nSSE-KMS with alias/s3-prod-key

note right of S3
S3 bucket default encryption

Objects uploaded to this bucket
can be encrypted using SSE-KMS
with the customer managed KMS key.

Bucket policy can also enforce
SSE-KMS by denying PutObject
requests that do not specify
aws:kms encryption.
end note

== Upload object with SSE-KMS ==

Principal -> S3: PutObject\nx-amz-server-side-encryption = aws:kms\nx-amz-server-side-encryption-aws-kms-key-id = alias/s3-prod-key

S3 -> IAM: Authorize s3:PutObject

note right of IAM
For upload with SSE-KMS, AWS evaluates:

1. Explicit Deny
2. SCPs
3. Permission boundary
4. IAM identity policy
5. S3 bucket policy
6. KMS key policy
7. KMS-related IAM permissions

The caller commonly needs:
- s3:PutObject
- kms:GenerateDataKey
end note

IAM --> S3: S3 write allowed

S3 -> KMS: GenerateDataKey\nfor customer managed KMS key

KMS -> IAM: Evaluate KMS permissions\nkms:GenerateDataKey

IAM -> KMS: Check key policy

alt#Gold #LightBlue KMS allowed
    KMS --> KMS: Key policy allows use
    KMS --> S3: Return plaintext data key\nand encrypted data key \nusing customer managed KMS master key
    S3 -> S3: Encrypt object using plaintext data key
    S3 -> S3: Store encrypted object\nand encrypted data key in metadata
    S3 -> CT: Log KMS and S3 activity
    S3 --> Principal: PutObject success
else  #Pink KMS denied
    KMS --> S3: AccessDenied
    S3 --> Principal: PutObject failed
end

== Download object encrypted with SSE-KMS ==

Principal -> S3: GetObject\nsigned with IAM principal credentials

S3 -> IAM: Authorize s3:GetObject

note right of IAM
For download with SSE-KMS, AWS evaluates:

1. Explicit Deny
2. SCPs
3. Permission boundary
4. IAM identity policy
5. S3 bucket policy
6. KMS key policy
7. KMS-related IAM permissions

The caller needs:
- s3:GetObject
- kms:Decrypt
end note

IAM --> S3: S3 read allowed

S3 -> KMS: Decrypt encrypted data key\nusing customer managed KMS master key

KMS -> IAM: Evaluate KMS permissions\nkms:Decrypt

IAM -> KMS: Check key policy
alt#Gold #LightBlue Decrypt allowed
    KMS --> KMS: Key policy allows decrypt
    KMS --> S3: Return plaintext data key
    S3 -> S3: Decrypt object using plaintext data key
    S3 -> S3: Remove plaintext data key from memory
    S3 -> CT: Log KMS Decrypt and S3 GetObject
    S3 --> Principal: Return decrypted object
else #Pink KMS Decrypt denied
    KMS --> S3: AccessDenied
    S3 --> Principal: AccessDenied
end

note right of KMS
1. KMS Responsible to store customer managed key or master key
2. KMS Responsible to decrypt the data key using master key
3. Encrypted data key is saved in S3 Object metadata
end note
@enduml